@startuml
title Snapshot File Replacement - Redis-like Server

' How a snapshot (or the AOF manifest) replaces the old file (SDD 13.2,
' DX-PER-2). The target path always names a complete file: the old one until
' the rename, the new one after it. Only the directory sync is left to make
' the rename itself survive a power loss.

[*] --> Temporary : create a new temporary file\nnext to the target\n(a leftover of the same name is removed first)

state Temporary : writing the serialised entries\ntarget: still the old, complete file\na reader sees the old file
state Synced : temporary file fsynced and closed\ntarget: still the old file\na reader sees the old file
state Renamed : temporary renamed over the target\ntarget: the new, complete file\na reader sees the new file
state Durable : directory fsynced\nthe rename survives a power loss\na reader sees the new file
state Abandoned : temporary file removed\ntarget: the old file, untouched\na reader sees the old file

Temporary --> Synced : write, flush, fsync\n(F_FULLFSYNC on macOS)
Synced --> Renamed : rename
Renamed --> Durable : fsync of the directory

Temporary --> Abandoned : write or sync fails
Synced --> Abandoned : rename fails
Renamed --> [*] : directory sync fails:\nerror reported, the new file is already in place
Durable --> [*] : success

Abandoned --> [*] : error reported\n(SAVE: ERR and the reason)

note right of Temporary
  A crash here leaves the old file and a stray
  temporary file; the next save removes it.
end note

@enduml
